|> Exercise
    handle=_dimensionality_t_over_v_

    If $x$ is a spatial displacement and $v$ is speed, what is
    the dimension of $x/v$?

    |> Solution

        It is time, since speed has the form
        “distance over time”, which cancels the “distance”
        dimension on top:

        $$
        {\te{DISTANCE}\over\te{SPEED}} = {\te{DISTANCE}\over \left({\te{DISTANCE} \over {\te{TIME}}}\right)} = {\te{DISTANCE}}\cdot {\te{TIME}\over \te{DISTANCE}} = \te{TIME}.
        $$
